ploited in prior attacks. Yet what is known about the LSU is only a few isolated mechanisms and parameters, and even a single one has been enough for an attack. In</description></item><item><title>DeepAVR: Deep Analysis via Multi-Agent Collaboration for Automated Vulnerability Repair</title><link>https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.32345</link><guid isPermaLink="false">5a180c5125b7</guid><pubDate>Wed, 25 Nov 2026 05:00:00 -0000</pubDate><category>untagged</category><description>arXiv cs.CR: arXiv:2610.32345v2 Announce Type: replace-cross Abstract: Automated Vulnerability Repair (AVR) has emerged as a promising approach to identifying and fixing software vulnerabilities autonomously. Early LLM-based approaches focused on patch generation under the assumption of perfect fault localization, while recent end-to-end methods attempt to repair vulnerabilities directly from sanitizer logs. H</description></item><item><title>From Attack Surface to Defense Architecture: Compositional Security for Agentic Coding Harnesses</title><link>https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.13960</link><guid isPermaLink="false">b61856a48aba</guid><pubDate>Wed, 25 Nov 2026 05:00:00 -0000</pubDate><category>untagged</category><description>arXiv cs.CR: arXiv:2611.13960v2 Announce Type: replace Abstract: AI coding harnesses couple language models to shells, filesystems, and network sockets so that agents can resolve issues and run tests. That coupling turns the harness into an automated Living-off-the-Land (LotL) platform: once an adversary can inject instructions into retrieved content, dual-use binaries such as bash, curl, and find become the p</description></item><item><title>Deletion Is Not Substitution: Component Attribution in a Layered Prompt-Injection Gateway for LLM Tool Calls</title><link>https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.33198</link><guid isPermaLink="false">3cc0a26d0bfe</guid><pubDate>Wed, 25 Nov 2026 05:00:00 -0000</pubDate><category>untagged</category><description>arXiv cs.CR: arXiv:2610.33198v2 Announce Type: r