false">6811e56c6c33</guid><pubDate>Wed, 25 Nov 2026 05:00:00 -0000</pubDate><category>untagged</category><description>arXiv cs.CR: arXiv:2602.06409v3 Announce Type: replace Abstract: Multimodal large language models (MLLMs) are pushing recommender systems (RecSys) toward content-grounded retrieval and ranking via cross-modal fusion. We find that while cross-modal consensus often mitigates conventional poisoning that manipulates interaction logs or perturbs a single modality, it also introduces a new attack surface where synch</description></item><item><title>SALSA: Sampling Adversarial Suffixes from Learned Distributions for LLM Safety Assessment</title><link>https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.33030</link><guid isPermaLink="false">bc9f635b28bc</guid><pubDate>Wed, 25 Nov 2026 05:00:00 -0000</pubDate><category>untagged</category><description>arXiv cs.CR: arXiv:2611.33030v1 Announce Type: cross Abstract: Large language models (LLMs) remain vulnerable to jailbreak attacks, where adversaries append optimized suffixes to otherwise benign looking prompts to bypass safety alignment. Existing optimization-based GCG-style attacks can be effective, but they are expensive at inference time, and suffer from poor transfer. We present, SALSA, a generative fram</description></item><item><title>&quot;I Do Intend to Deliver&quot;: Will LLMs Run a Marketplace Advance-Fee Scam?</title><link>https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.32957</link><guid isPermaLink="false">25718390b6b1</guid><pubDate>Wed, 25 Nov 2026 05:00:00 -0000</pubDate><category>untagged</category><description>arXiv cs.CR: arXiv:2611.32957v1 Announce Type: cross Abstract: Online fraud is traditionally limited by the cost of the human labor needed to talk to potential victims. We ask whether hosted large language models will now supply that labor. We prompted ten off-the-shelf models to play the seller in the &quot;free item, just pay delivery&quot; advance-fee scam common on buy-and-sell sites, using a plain system prompt wit</description></item><item><title>CAPTA: Identifying LLMs Behind AI Agents</title><link>https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.32686</link><guid isPermaLink="false">875a747b9f5b</guid><pubDate>Wed, 25 Nov 2026 05:00:00 -0000</pubDate><category>untagged</category><description>arXiv cs.CR: arXiv:2611.32686v1 Announce Type: cross Abstract: Large language model (LLM)-based agents access remote services through tools to complete user tasks, often via API calls. In this setting, a remote service may want to identify the backend LLM of an agent to launch model-specific attacks, including prompt injection or selective delivery of manipulated content. However, the service cannot directly q</description></item><item><title>BODYBENCH: Evaluating Adversarial Image Defenses Against AI Nudification Inpainting</title><link>https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.31774</link><guid isPermaLink="false">506735e9529a</guid><pubDate>Wed, 25 Nov 2026 05:00:00 -0000</pubDate><category>untagged</category><description>arXiv cs.CR: arXiv:2611.31774v1 Announce Type: cross Abstract: Adversarial perturbation defenses protect images from unauthorized edits by adding imperceptible noise. Yet none of these methods have been evaluated against nudification, despite AI-generated non-consensual intimate imagery (AIG-NCII) being among the most documented harms of generative AI. Moreover, nudification edits are readily carried out throu</description></item></channel></rss>